    "result_text": "Done. The full build is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests passing).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `contactPreference` field returns `'EMAIL'` when an owner has an email, otherwise `'PHONE'`. I followed the codebase's existing pattern of small, cohesive derived-value helpers (`Locality`, `MembershipLevel`, `BulkSignupWarning`).\n\nChanges (3 files):\n\n1. **`ContactPreference.java`** (new) \u2014 a tiny, self-contained helper with a single static `of(Owner)` method (CC 2, zero surrounding context). Mirrors `Locality`/`MembershipLevel`.\n\n2. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `contactPreference` string property to the `Owner` schema so the generated `OwnerDto` gets its field and `setContactPreference` setter.\n\n3. **`RespondWithOwner.java`** \u2014 one added line: `dto.setContactPreference(ContactPreference.of(owner))`, next to the sibling derived-field calls.\n\nStructural impact was kept minimal: the only pre-existing function edited (`RespondWithOwner.service`) is a straight-line method with no other methods in its class (WMC_other \u2248 0, CC 1, 1 changed line). All the new logic lives in a fresh single-method class, so it contributes no editing cost to any large class. I put the derivation only in the GET responder (`RespondWithOwner`) \u2014 the responder the spec's tests exercise \u2014 matching the existing convention where each responder sets its own subset of derived fields (e.g. `RespondWithOwnerCreated` already omits `locality`).",
